The Python binding gives you `Session` and `Host` with Python values over
`ctypes`
([`glowcap`](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/python/glowcap)).
`Runtime.open` holds one Host and the transports you install, and
`runtime.connect` picks the one that matches the locator. The transport modules
include `glowcap.iroh`, `glowcap.onion`, `glowcap.relay`, and
`glowcap.tcp`. Start from the
[checkout instructions]($link.page('bindings')).

# The same round-trip

This is the [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip in Python. `fetch`
and `send` start the inbound loop on demand, so a client that only makes calls
never needs `serve`.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:dial:python GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **iroh**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Runtime
> from glowcap.iroh import IrohConfig, Reachability, locator, provider
>
> # DISCOVERY resolves a bare iroh node ID without an address hint.
> node_id = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>"
> partner = locator(node_id)
>
> async with Runtime.open(
>     provider=provider(IrohConfig(Reachability.DISCOVERY))
> ) as runtime:
> ```
> **webrtc**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Runtime
> from glowcap.webrtc import SignalingAuthority, locator, provider
>
> # Your signaling carries the offer and each candidate to the peer the locator
> # names, over a path you already hold, such as an OCapN capability both peers
> # can reach, and brings the answer and the peer's candidates back.
> signaling: SignalingAuthority
> partner = locator("<64 lowercase hex characters from the peer>")
>
> async with Runtime.open(provider=provider(signaling)) as runtime:
> ```
> **noise-tcp**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Runtime, tcp
>
> # Same TCP sockets as keyed dialing; Noise_XK seals the CapTP stream.
> peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>"
> partner = tcp.noise.locator(peer_key, "127.0.0.1", 9099)
>
> async with Runtime.open(provider=tcp.provider()) as runtime:
> ```
> **noise-relay**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Runtime, relay
>
> # Noise_XK seals the CapTP stream end to end through the relay.
> peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>"
> relay_url = "wss://relay.example/relay"
> partner = relay.noise.locator(peer_key, relay_url)
>
> async with Runtime.open(provider=relay.provider(relay_url)) as runtime:
> ```
> **onion**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Runtime, onion
>
> # glowcap starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> partner = onion.locator("<56-character onion service id>")
>
> async with Runtime.open(
>     provider=onion.provider(onion.OnionConfig(socks="/run/tor/socks"))
> ) as runtime:
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Runtime, tcp
>
> # Key designator, cleartext CapTP. Partners dial the public key; the host:port is a hint.
> peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>"
> partner = tcp.keyed.locator(peer_key, "127.0.0.1", 9099)
>
> async with Runtime.open(provider=tcp.provider()) as runtime:
> ```
> **keyed-relay**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Runtime, relay
>
> # The key names the peer; the hint names the relay that can reach it.
> peer_key = "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>"
> relay_url = "wss://relay.example/relay"
> partner = relay.keyed.locator(peer_key, relay_url)
>
> async with Runtime.open(provider=relay.provider(relay_url)) as runtime:
> ```
> **tcp**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Runtime, tcp
>
> # Plain TCP uses a cleartext host and port.
> partner = tcp.locator("127.0.0.1", 9099)
>
> async with Runtime.open(provider=tcp.provider()) as runtime:
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```python
    async with await runtime.connect(partner) as session:
        greeter = await session.fetch("greeter")  # a capability this peer published
        reply = await session.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"])
        print(reply)
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:dial:python -->
```

The iroh tab needs the optional
transport library and a discovery-reachable node id.

# Listen

`tcp.listen` binds a port and accepts sessions that publish and serve.
`tcp.keyed.listen` and `tcp.noise.listen` take a 32-byte seed so the advertised
designator survives a restart. `iroh.listen` binds a node id instead.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:listen:python GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **iroh**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Argument
> from glowcap.iroh import IrohConfig, Reachability, listen
>
> # This listener is named by its node id. Partners can dial it from anywhere.
> async with await listen(IrohConfig(Reachability.DISCOVERY)) as server:
> ```
> **noise-tcp**
> ```python
> import os
> from glowcap import Argument, tcp
>
> # Same bind as keyed-tcp; Noise_XK seals every accepted CapTP stream.
> # Persist the 32-byte seed to keep the same key.
> seed = os.urandom(32)
> async with await tcp.noise.listen("127.0.0.1", 9099, seed=seed) as server:
> ```
> **noise-relay**
> ```python
> import os
> from glowcap import Argument, relay
>
> # Register this key at the relay; Noise_XK seals every accepted CapTP stream.
> seed = os.urandom(32)  # load durable secret bytes in a real service
> async with await relay.noise.listen(
>     "wss://relay.example/relay",
>     "wss://relay.example/register",
>     seed=seed,
> ) as server:
> ```
> **onion**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Argument, onion
>
> # glowcap starts no Tor. Name an already-running daemon's SOCKS (and control to host).
> async with await onion.listen("/run/tor/socks", control="/run/tor/control") as server:
>     service_key = server.service_key  # persist securely to restore this service
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```python
> import os
> from glowcap import Argument, tcp
>
> # Key designator, cleartext CapTP. Persist the 32-byte seed to keep the same key.
> seed = os.urandom(32)
> async with await tcp.keyed.listen("127.0.0.1", 9099, seed=seed) as server:
> ```
> **tcp**
> ```python
> from glowcap import Argument, tcp
>
> # Plain TCP binds the host and port you choose.
> async with await tcp.listen("127.0.0.1", 9099) as server:
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```python
    print(server.uri)  # hand this locator to partners

    def greet(verb: str, args: list[Argument]) -> str:
        if verb != "greet":
            raise LookupError("no such method")
        if len(args) != 1 or not isinstance(args[0], str):
            raise TypeError("bad invocation")
        return f"Hello, {args[0]}"

    server.host.publish("greeter", greet)
    async for accepted in server:
        async with accepted as session:
            await session.serve()
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:listen:python -->
```

# Async

The binding uses
[`asyncio`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio.html).
`await session.send(...)` returns the reply, and `session.serve()` runs
the inbound loop as a background task until the session closes.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers the CapTP ideas.

# Actors

`session.spawn` and `session.publish` take a `behavior(verb, args)` callable. If the callable raises, the caller's promise breaks with the exception's message. To let a third peer
reach an actor directly through an introduction, call `publish` on the `Host`
from `server.host`.

The complete API lives in the
[Python binding README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/python/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/clients/python/room_client.py)
shows the rest.
